Azure Dreams is one of a class of Japanese Roguelike-wannabes that, at
one time, were almost popular.

There are a good number of these, either as partial games (the
Chrysler Building in Parasite Eve, the Ancient Cave in all Lufias
after the first) or complete games in their own right (Fatal
Labyrinth, Azure Dreams, Chocobo's Mysterious Dungeon, the Dragon
Warrior Monsters series, Timestalkers, and others).

It is insightful to examine them and note that they are obviously
inspired by Roguelikes, or more likely inspired by something that was
inspired by Roguelikes, but are not actually Roguelikes, and to
determine why they are not.

What they usually have:
* Randomized dungeons
* Randomly-placed items
* Some variation on permanent death gameplay, but no actually
permanent death.  (You may lose all levels but keep items, or lose all
items or keep levels, or lose both but your Pokemon-like monster pets
get to retain development, etc.)
* Often they have cursed items, or items that must be idenfitied, but
implemented in a way that causes them to oh-so-subtlely miss the
point.

What they usually don't have:
* The true item-ID system of Roguelikes.  Unknown items are usually
just a typical item with an "unknown" flag, and identifying them
merely removes the flag and tells the player what it is.  Knowledge
rarely persists between items, and anyway the typical Roguelike magic
items (potions, scrolls, rings/amulets and wands) are very often not
unknowns.
* The rougelike movement and combat system.  This is awsomely
important, as movement, fleeing and combat are all elements of the
same interface.  Most Japanese RPGs move to a separate screen, which
destroys a lot of the strategy inherent in Roguelike games.
* The monsters typically have nowhere near the character of Roguelike
monsters.  Floating eyes would never make it in that kind of game.
* The amazing degree of item relationships is missing -- you'd never
be able to do things like dip items in holy water, the concept is too
subtle for these games.
* The games are typically balanced too well.  This is important, part
of Nethack's charm, and budding game designers please take note -- * a
game that's selectively unbalanced can be a lot more fun than a
balanced one! *  There would never be something like a cockatrice
corpse in these games, that arbitrarily allows you to defeat monsters
for a while.  Sure it's dangerous, and Nethack is indeed balanced
because of that, but it's a different type of balance from Japanese
RPGs.

In other words, to paraphrase the Paranoia manual, 2nd edition:
Nethack is cool.  Those other games are not cool.  Play Nethack.

